<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">South African Border War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1966">1966</a>–<a href="/years/1990">1990</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Namibian War of Independence · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The South African Border War was fought from 1966 to 1990 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> and the Namibian liberation movement <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swapo">SWAPO</a>, with Angolan and Cuban forces involved after 1975. It ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york-accords">New York Accords</a> of 1988, which linked Cuban withdrawal from Angola with Namibian independence, achieved in 1990.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/south-african-border-war.jpg" alt="South African Border War" width="320" height="341" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Collage: Katangais. Individual constituent images used in the collage: Smikect, Chris Lofting, Allen Herweg, Sam van den Berg and … · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SABorder_War_Montage2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a> was governed as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-south-west-africa">German South West Africa</a> until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, when it was invaded and occupied by Allied forces under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-botha">Louis Botha</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-11-november-1918">Armistice of 11 November 1918</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> placed it under a mandate. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> superseded the League, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-smuts">Jan Smuts</a>, who led the South African delegation, distrusted the proposed trusteeship system because of the vague wording of Article 77 of the Charter. Mounting opposition to apartheid inspired a South West African nationalist movement in the 1950s, and in 1955 students and teachers formed the South West African Progressive Association under Uatja Kaukuetu.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> and Liberia petitioned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-court-of-justice">International Court of Justice</a> in November 1960, and on 21 December 1962 the court ruled that they had the right to bring the case. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swapo">SWAPO</a> formed its armed wing, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-liberation-army">People's Liberation Army</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a> (PLAN), in 1962 with help from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and sympathetic African states such as Tanzania, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Fighting between PLAN and the South African security forces broke out in August 1966. PLAN grew more confident in 1973: on 26 January about 50 insurgents attacked a police base at Singalamwe in the Caprivi with mortars, machine guns and a rocket launcher, and by the end of the year the insurgency had spread across Caprivi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovamboland">Ovamboland</a>, Kaokoland and Kavangoland. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carnation-revolution">Carnation Revolution</a> in Portugal of 25 April 1974 then destabilised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>. South Africa feared a Soviet-backed government there allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swapo">SWAPO</a>, and in 1975 its forces became involved in Angola as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-intelligence-agency">Central Intelligence Agency</a> armed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-liberation-front-of-angola">FNLA</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpla">MPLA</a> received Soviet arms through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>.</p><p>From 1975 to 1988 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-african-defence-force">South African Defence Force</a> staged large conventional raids into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a> against PLAN bases, and used counter-insurgency units such as Koevoet and 32 Battalion. In 1978 P. W. Botha succeeded Vorster as prime minister and made Cuban withdrawal from Angola a condition of Namibian independence, a policy the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> supported and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swapo">SWAPO</a> denounced. In 1987 the Angolan army launched Operation Saluting October against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unita">UNITA</a>, and the fighting around Cuito Cuanavale caused alarm in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> because of its casualties.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The South African government concluded in 1988 that it had reached the political limits of further escalation, and war weariness had grown among its civilians. The 1988 Tripartite Accord and the terms of Security Council Resolution 435 provided the basis for a settlement: elections for a constitutional assembly, confinement of PLAN and the Defence Force to their bases, withdrawal of all but 1,500 South African troops, demobilisation of paramilitary forces and the return of refugees. The United Nations Transition Assistance Group was to supervise the process, and the war ended on 21 March 1990.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “South African Border War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 1966 the army fought the South West African People’s Organisation in the <b>South African Border War</b>, which spread into Angola after 1975, when South African forces intervened in the Angolan civil war against the MPLA government and its Cuban allies.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-south-africa">Republic of South Africa · Military</a></li></ul></section>
